.TH BRIEF 1 "2026-08-01" "brief 0.1.0" "General Commands Manual"
.SH NAME
brief \- find the skill that fits a task
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B brief ls
.RI [ ref ]
.br
.B brief cat
.IR ref ...
.br
.B brief find
.RI [ flags ]
.IR task ...
.br
.B brief lint
.RI [ flags ]
.RI [ path ...]
.br
.B brief new
.RI [ flags ]
.I name
.br
.B brief path
.RI [ flags ]
.I name
.br
.B brief prompt
.RI [ flags ]
.br
.B brief version
.br
.B brief help
.SH DESCRIPTION
A skill is a directory holding a
.IR SKILL.md :
YAML frontmatter saying what it does and when to use it, then Markdown
instructions. That is the Agent Skills specification, and it is all
.B brief
knows.
.PP
.B brief
is the catalogue of those directories, as a filter. It lists them, it
chooses between them, and it prints one. It does not install a skill, run
one, or wrap an agent around one; what it prints is text, and what happens
to that text is the shell\(aqs business. The companion program
.BR ask (1)
is the one that needs a model.
.PP
The specification asks agents to disclose a skill progressively: names and
descriptions at startup, one skill\(aqs instructions when it is chosen, and
its bundled files only when they are needed. Those three levels are three
commands here.
.B brief ls
is level one,
.B brief cat
of a name is level two, and
.B brief cat
of a path inside a skill is level three. Nothing reads a level that was not
asked for, and
.B brief find \-ask
sends level one and never anything else.
.PP
A
.I ref
is a skill name, or a name and a path inside that skill, as in
.IR pdf\-processing/references/FORMS.md .
Names resolve on
.SM $BRIEF_PATH
left to right, first match wins, exactly like
.SM $PATH.
A name may hold only lowercase letters, digits and single hyphens, so it
can never be a path; anything else is read as a filesystem path, and the
skill is then the nearest directory at or above it holding a
.IR SKILL.md .
That is what lets
.B brief lint .
and
.B brief cat ./draft
work on a skill that is not installed anywhere yet.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B brief ls \fR[\fIref\fR]
With no argument, print the catalogue: one skill per line, as a name, a
tab, and its description flattened to a single line. This is level one, it
is what
.B find \-ask
puts in front of a model, and it is the shape
.BR cut (1)
reads. With a
.IR ref ,
print the files that skill bundles instead, as paths relative to the skill,
sorted \(em exactly the paths
.B brief cat
will accept.
.TP
.B brief cat \fIref\fR...
Print instructions. A bare name gives the body with the frontmatter
stripped, which is what belongs in a prompt: the name and description were
how the skill was chosen, and repeating them spends context the
instructions are about to need. Naming the file instead
.RI ( name/SKILL.md )
gives the file, byte for byte. Several refs are concatenated, because it is
.BR cat (1).
A path inside a skill may not leave that skill.
.TP
.B brief find \fR[\fIflags\fR] \fItask\fR...
Print the name of the skill that fits a task, best first, and exit 1 if
nothing does. The task is the arguments joined with spaces; with no
arguments it is read from standard input, when standard input is not a
terminal. Arguments win outright rather than composing with standard input,
so
.B find
inside a loop that inherited a pipe cannot wait forever for a task nobody
meant to send.
.IP
By default the ranking is a weighted word match over names and
descriptions: a word only one skill uses decides, a word most of them use
is discarded rather than scored low, and a skill named outright in the task
wins. It is free, instant and offline, and it says nothing rather than
guessing.
.B \-ask
hands the choice to a model instead, by running
.BR ask (1).
.TP
.B brief lint \fR[\fIflags\fR] [\fIpath\fR...]
Check skills against the specification and print what is wrong, one finding
per line, in the shape a compiler uses. An argument may be a skill
directory, a directory of skills, or the
.I SKILL.md
itself; with no argument every skill on
.SM $BRIEF_PATH
is checked.
.IP
Errors are violations of a rule: a name that is not the directory name, a
name over 64 characters or holding anything but lowercase letters, digits
and single hyphens, a description that is missing, empty, or over 1024
characters, a
.I compatibility
over 500 characters, a
.I metadata
that is not a mapping of strings, an
.I allowed\-tools
that is not one string, a duplicate key that YAML silently drops, an empty
body, and a file the instructions reference that is not there.
.IP
Warnings are the specification\(aqs advice and its silences: an unknown
frontmatter field, a body past 500 lines or roughly 5000 tokens, a
description that says what a skill does but never when to use it, a
description long enough to be expensive at startup, a reference more than
one level deep, and a bundled file no instruction ever mentions \(em which
no agent will open, because no agent reads a directory it was not told
about.
.TP
.B brief new \fR[\fIflags\fR] \fIname\fR
Write a new skill and print the path to its
.IR SKILL.md .
The scaffold passes
.B brief lint \-strict
as written, because the first file an author sees teaches them the shape.
An existing file is never overwritten without
.BR \-f .
.TP
.B brief path \fR[\fIflags\fR] \fIname\fR
Print the directory a name resolves to, or exit 1 if it resolves to
nothing. With
.B \-a
print every directory on the path that holds a skill of that name, nearest
first, which is the only way to tell a shadowed skill from an ignored one.
.TP
.B brief prompt \fR[\fIflags\fR]
Print the system prompt
.B find \-ask
sends. It is a value rather than a secret, so extending it is ordinary
shell:
.B brief find \-ask
uses this text, and
.B brief prompt | cat \- house\-rules
is how you would build a longer one.
.TP
.B brief version
Print the version.
.B \-V
and
.B \-\-version
do the same.
.TP
.B brief help
Print a summary of everything above.
.B \-h
and
.B \-\-help
do the same. Help that was asked for goes to standard output; a misuse goes
to standard error and leaves standard output empty.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS find
.TP
.B \fB\-ask\fR
Choose with a model, by running
.BR ask (1),
instead of by matching words. The catalogue goes on
.BR ask (1)\(aqs
standard input and the task in its arguments. The run is a fresh session of
its own under
.SM $BRIEF_DIR/find
\(em never the conversation the caller is having \(em and its path is
printed on standard error, so
.B ask replay \-check
can prove the choice afterwards. A name that is not in the catalogue is
refused rather than opened.
.TP
.B \fB\-n\fR \fInum\fR
Print at most
.I num
names, best first. The default is 1.
.TP
.B \fB\-v\fR
Explain the ranking on standard error: every candidate, its score, and the
words that earned it.
.TP
.B \fB\-q\fR
No progress on standard error.
.SS lint
.TP
.B \fB\-strict\fR
Treat warnings as errors, so a build fails on advice. This is what
continuous integration wants; a working directory usually does not.
.TP
.B \fB\-q\fR
Print nothing. The exit status is the answer.
.SS new
.TP
.B \fB\-d\fR \fIdir\fR
Create the skill under
.IR dir .
The default is the working directory.
.TP
.B \fB\-f\fR
Overwrite an existing
.IR SKILL.md .
.SS path
.TP
.B \fB\-a\fR
Print every directory holding the name, not just the first.
.SS prompt
.TP
.B \fB\-n\fR \fInum\fR
The limit the printed prompt states, matching
.B find \-n
.IR num .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.SM BRIEF_PATH
Where skills live: a list of directories separated by the path list
separator, searched left to right, first match wins. Empty elements are
skipped rather than read as the working directory. The default is
.IR .claude/skills ,
.IR ~/.claude/skills ,
.IR ~/.brief/skills .
.TP
.SM BRIEF_MODEL
The model
.B find \-ask
uses, passed to
.BR ask (1)
as
.BR \-m .
Unset, the model is whatever
.BR ask (1)
would use on its own. Set it when selection should run on something cheaper
than the model doing the work.
.TP
.SM BRIEF_DIR
Where
.B brief
keeps what it owns. The only thing in it is the
.BR ask (1)
session behind each
.B \-ask
choice, under
.IR find/ .
The default is
.IR ~/.brief .
.TP
.SM ASK
The
.BR ask (1)
binary to run. The default is
.IR ask ,
found on
.SM $PATH.
A wrapper that pins a cheap model belongs here.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <skill>/SKILL.md
The skill: frontmatter and instructions. Required.
.TP
.I <skill>/scripts/, <skill>/references/, <skill>/assets/
The specification\(aqs bundled directories: level three.
.B brief
lists them and prints them, and never runs them.
.TP
.I ~/.brief/find/*.jsonl
One
.BR ask (1)
session per
.B find \-ask
choice, kept so the choice can be read back.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Yes. A skill was found, a listing was not empty, a check was clean.
.TP
.B 1
No. Nothing matched, a name resolved to nothing, or
.B lint
had something to say. This is an ordinary answer, not a failure, and a
script should branch on it.
.TP
.B 2
Error. A bad invocation, an unreadable directory, a reference that escapes
its skill, or
.BR ask (1)
failing.
.PP
This is the contract of
.BR grep (1)
rather than that of
.BR ask (1),
because
.B brief
asks a question where no is a real answer, and something being broken has
to be distinguishable from it.
.SH EXAMPLES
Choose a skill and hand it to
.BR ask (1)
as a system prompt, keeping ask\(aqs own default underneath it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ask \-S "$(ask system; brief cat pdf\-processing)" "pull the tables from q3.pdf"
.fi
.RE
.PP
The same thing for whatever task arrives, as a shell function:
.PP
.RS
.nf
skilled() {
  s=$(brief find \-ask \-q "$*") || { ask "$@"; return; }
  ask \-S "$(ask system; brief cat "$s")" "$@"
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Progressive disclosure by hand, which is all
.B find \-ask
automates:
.PP
.RS
.nf
brief ls | ask \-q "which of these fits \(dq$task\(dq? name only"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Keep a project honest, in continuous integration:
.PP
.RS
.nf
brief lint \-strict .claude/skills
.fi
.RE
.PP
Print the level\-three files a skill bundles, then read one:
.PP
.RS
.nf
brief ls web\-perf
brief cat web\-perf/references/metrics.md
.fi
.RE
.PP
Feed a skill to an agent that is not
.BR ask (1):
.PP
.RS
.nf
claude \-\-append\-system\-prompt "$(brief cat code\-review)" \-p "review this diff"
.fi
.RE
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Findings from
.B lint
go to standard output, because they are what was asked for; everything else
.B brief
says about its own progress goes to standard error. A run that found
nothing prints nothing to standard output, so a name and a diagnostic can
never be confused by whatever is reading the pipe.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ask (1),
.BR grep (1),
.BR cut (1),
.BR xargs (1)
.PP
The Agent Skills specification: https://agentskills.io/specification
.SH CAVEATS
.B brief find
without
.B \-ask
matches words. It cannot know that a scanned invoice is a PDF problem, and
it will say so by printing nothing rather than by guessing.
.PP
.B brief find \-ask
costs a model call and takes about a second. In a loop over many tasks that
is the wall clock; rank without it first, and fall back.
.PP
Anything a model writes is untrusted.
.B brief
checks the name it gets back against the catalogue, but the instructions a
skill contains were written by whoever wrote the skill, and
.B brief cat
prints them without judgement.
